  "summary": "The biblical claim that humans are created to represent God's rule, bear dignity, and exercise wise stewardship in relationship with God and neighbor.",
  "historical_context": "Genesis places the image of God at creation and carries the language into the post-fall human story, where vocation remains but is marred.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "Royal imagery was common in the ancient world, but Genesis extends representative dignity to humanity as a whole rather than only to kings.",
  "hebraic_worldview": "Human vocation, relational responsibility, dignity, stewardship, and moral accountability.",
  "second_temple_context": "Second Temple Jewish reflection connected the image of God with wisdom, holiness, and the hope of restored humanity.",
  "canonical_context": "The image is damaged in sin, echoed in covenant ethics, and renewed in Christ, the true image, and in the new humanity.",
  "later_christian_reception": "Christian anthropology, ethics, and theology have used the image theme to discuss dignity, fall, sanctification, and vocation.",
